{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["The reason for <b>and you shall count for yourselves</b> is like \"and you shall take for yourselves\" (Leviticus 23:40) — that the counting and the taking should be for each and every individual, that he count with his mouth and recite the tally, as our Rabbis received. It is not so with \"and he shall count for himself\" (Leviticus 15:13) and \"she shall count for herself\" (v. 28) regarding one with a discharge, for if they wish they may remain in their impurity; rather, it is only that they not forget it. And likewise \"and you shall count for yourself\" (Leviticus 25:8) regarding the Jubilee, that you be careful with the number so that you not forget. And in Torat Kohanim (Behar, parashah 2:1), \"and you shall count for yourself\" — by the court. And I do not know whether to say that the Great Court are obligated to count years and sabbatical cycles at the beginning of each year and to recite a blessing over them, as is done in the counting of the Omer, or to say that the court should be careful with the count and sanctify the fiftieth year. Now, the number of days from the day of the waving until the day of sacred convocation is like the number of years from the sabbatical years until the Jubilee, and the reason for them is one; therefore \"you shall count fifty days\" — that he count seven weeks, forty-nine days, and sanctify the fiftieth day, which is counted by hand, as he said regarding the Jubilee. And this is the reason for \"they shall be complete\" — that they be precise, neither less nor more, like the reason for \"they shall be complete\" (Numbers 28:19), since the excess is like the deficiency: it is not complete. Now, the reason for the passage is that he begin to count at the beginning of the barley harvest and bring the first offering of his harvest, a fresh grain meal-offering to HaShem, and offer upon it a sacrifice; and he completes its count at the beginning of the wheat harvest, when the sheaf is ready in its season, and from it he brings fine flour of wheat, a meal-offering to HaShem (Leviticus 23:16–17), and brings a sacrifice upon it (vv. 18–20). Therefore he mentioned these sacrifices in the passage, because they are on account of the meal-offerings, which are the essential element of this festival, and he did not mention the additional offerings in connection with them, just as he did not mention them in connection with the rest of the festivals."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The phrase כרמל (v. 14) and לחם (v. 17) are rendered literally as 'fresh grain' and 'bread' respectively, following the context of grain offerings."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:26.778749446Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}